<h2>The decree as a boundary, not a full stop</h2>
<p>A decree of divorce under the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 does one thing with certainty: it dissolves the marital status from the date the decree operates. Everything else is consequence — some of it automatic, some of it available on application, and some of it entirely dependent on the parties remembering to act. Litigants who have spent years securing the decree frequently neglect the quieter work that follows it, and discover the omission at the worst possible moment: at a remarriage, at a succession dispute, or at the counter of an insurer processing a claim in favour of a former spouse. What follows is the after-decree map, consequence by consequence.</p>

<h2>Money: Section 25 outlives the decree</h2>
<p>Permanent alimony is dealt with in detail elsewhere on this site, so a single point bears repetition here: Section 25 is not confined to the moment of decree. Alimony may be sought <em>at the time of passing any decree or at any time subsequent thereto</em>, and an order once made may be varied under Section 25(2) on proof of changed circumstances of either party, or rescinded under Section 25(3) in the circumstances that subsection sets out, including remarriage of the recipient. The financial chapter, in other words, can be reopened in both directions long after the marriage has closed.</p>

<h2>Remarriage: the Section 15 timetable</h2>
<p>Section 15, likewise covered in its own explainer, fixes when remarriage becomes lawful: where there is no right of appeal against the decree, where the appeal period has expired without an appeal, or where an appeal has been presented and dismissed. The practical discipline is simple — diarise the appeal period, obtain a certified copy of the decree, and verify that no appeal is pending before solemnising a second marriage.</p>

<h2>Succession: what the decree severs, and what it spares</h2>
<p>Heirship between spouses under the Hindu Succession Act, 1956 attaches to a subsisting marriage. Once the marriage stands dissolved, the former spouses cease to be heirs to one another: neither takes on the other’s intestacy, however long the marriage lasted or however recent the decree. Two things, however, are untouched. First, the children of the marriage remain heirs of both parents; divorce rearranges the household, not the bloodline. Second, instruments speak for themselves: a will executed during the marriage in favour of the then spouse is not rewritten by the decree and continues to operate per its terms until the testator changes it. A party who does not wish a former spouse to take under an old will must make a new one.</p>

<h2>Records, nominations and instruments</h2>
<p>Updating records after the decree is prudent practice rather than automatic legal effect, and the distinction matters. A nomination generally identifies who may receive a payment; to whom the money ultimately belongs is a question of succession, and the two can diverge. Professional advice on each specific instrument is therefore worth its cost.</p>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h4>Insurance policies</h4><p>Review nominations and, where a policy was assigned or taken for the benefit of the spouse, examine its terms; the policy document governs, and insurers act on the records before them.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Provident fund and pension</h4><p>EPF, gratuity and pension records carry their own nomination forms and family definitions under their governing schemes; each should be updated through the employer or the fund, not assumed to follow the decree.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Bank accounts and wills</h4><p>Joint accounts, lockers and standing nominations need fresh mandates, and an existing will should be revisited in full — codicils that merely delete a name often create more disputes than they resolve.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What continues by design</h2>
<p>Some consequences of the matrimonial litigation are built to survive the decree. Obligations recorded in a settlement deed or consent terms — staggered payments, transfer of articles, withdrawal of proceedings — survive according to their own terms, and where the terms are incorporated in the decree they carry the enforceability of the decree itself. Recovery of stridhan and personal effects likewise remains available after divorce, a subject covered separately on this site. And custody or visitation arrangements continue in operation while remaining permanently provisional: Section 26 of the Hindu Marriage Act empowers the court to make, vary and revoke orders concerning children <em>from time to time</em>, even after the decree, with the welfare of the child as the constant lodestar. No custody clause, however carefully negotiated, is beyond revision when circumstances change.</p>

<h2>The after-decree checklist</h2>
<div class="check"><ul>
<li>Obtain certified copies of the decree and judgment; confirm whether any appeal has been filed before acting on finality.</li>
<li>Diarise the appeal period before planning remarriage under Section 15.</li>
<li>Assess whether a claim, variation or rescission under Section 25 is warranted now or foreseeable later; preserve financial records either way.</li>
<li>Make or update a will; do not rely on the decree to displace an old one.</li>
<li>Update nominations and records — insurance, EPF, gratuity, pension, bank accounts, lockers — through each institution’s own process.</li>
<li>Calendar every obligation in the settlement deed or consent terms, with proof of compliance retained for each.</li>
<li>Keep custody and visitation arrangements documented, and seek variation under Section 26 rather than acting unilaterally when circumstances change.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="note"><p>The decree settles status; diligence settles everything else. Most post-divorce disputes in practice arise not from the decree but from the unattended paperwork around it — an old nomination, an unrevised will, an undocumented payment. How these consequences play out depends on the instruments and facts involved, and independent legal advice on specific documents is always advisable.</p></div>
